<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/054Q">Example <span data-tag="054Q">3.5.7.2</span>.</a> Let $n$ be an integer. Recall that a Kan complex $X$ is <em>$(n+1)$-coskeletal</em> if, for every integer $m \geq n+2$, every morphism of simplicial sets $\operatorname{\partial \Delta }^{m} \rightarrow X$ extends <em>uniquely</em> to an $m$-simplex of $X$ (Definition <a data-tag="0520" href="/tag/0520">3.5.3.1</a>). If this condition is satisfied, then $X$ is $n$-truncated. In particular, every $n$-groupoid is $n$-truncated (Corollary <a data-tag="053X" href="/tag/053X">3.5.5.11</a>). See Proposition <a data-tag="0553" href="/tag/0553">3.5.7.16</a> (or Variant <a data-tag="0554" href="/tag/0554">3.5.7.17</a>) for a partial converse. </p>
